<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artoces</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 65 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artoces was king of Caucasian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> in the first century BCE. In 65 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> pursued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates VI of Pontus</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, Artoces resisted and was defeated in battles on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Mtkvari</a> (Kura) and Aragvi rivers, after which he submitted and gave his children as hostages. He is known from Greek and Roman writers such as Appian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>.</p></div>
